descriptions:
Research in higher income countries suggests that approximately 4% of women will develop post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) after a traumatic birth. Research in lower-middle income countries is sparse but suggests higher rates of PTSD after birth. The INTERSECT study was carried out in 31 countries to understand rates of birth-related PTSD, however, only 2 countries were based in Sub-Saharan Africa. This is a large research gap given the high levels of maternal mortality and birth injury rates found in these countries. This networking grant will therefore lay the groundwork for INTERSECT Africa, which will bring together a network of researchers in African countries who aim to find out about rates and causes of birth-related PTSD. This will eventually lead to changes in maternity care, improving the outcomes of mothers. 

We will lay the groundwork for INTERSECT Africa in this project by: 
(i) Strengthening research relationships between the UK and Malawi through in-person meetings, mentoring, and an INTERSECT conference; 
(ii)  Build the network by identifying other African countries who are interested in participating in INTERSECT Africa and; 
(iii) Applying for funding to carry out INTERSECT in interested African countries.

The fellowships will support research at the University of Birmingham on topics related to Forest Ecology, Biodiversity, and Conservation; Energy Transition; and Public Health and Health Systems in the Brazilian Amazon.

Specific project topics can include:
* impact of drought and drier conditions on tropical forest systems and biodiversity, adaptation and response of forest systems to a changing climate, or vulnerabilities specific to the ecosystems of the Brazilian Amazon;
* challenges around decarbonisation of road transport and the impact on the local and national economies and infrastructure in Brazil;
* health systems and resilience, including the needs of vulnerable populations, the presence of environmental contaminants, and the nexus of food-water-energy in Brazilian Amazon settings.

The university will recruit three early career fellows from the Philippines, who will be working on projects centred on the theme of Resilient Planet, focusing on interdisciplinary research on the three topics outlined below.  The projects, which will run for 9 months, will investigate how artificial intelligence (AI)-based approaches for analysing unstructured data (i.e., large amounts of text) can be applied to these critical issues to help the vulnerable and low-income populations in the Philippines.
Agri-tech and health (food systems): the understanding of food production and consumption-related information in order to make consumer diets healthier and more sustainable;
Biodiversity conservation: the extraction of scientific knowledge that can inform biodiversity conservation programmes;
Risk reduction from extreme weather: the real-time detection of impacts of natural disasters on citizens based on social media content.
